s resilience and crew cooperation for A, or maybe bring up candidate C or D. But we need to keep focused. She could agree with James, maintain recommendation for A, but note need for more info. Or she could propose to discuss candidate C or D. But the instruction says each turn should be focused argumentative contribution, referencing prior discussion. She could say \"I agree with James, still recommend A, but we need to probe stress resilience and crew cooperation further; also we should now discuss candidate C's decision speed and attention skills vs his communication issues.\" That would be a next step.\n\nWe need to include explicit candidate facts: A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, leadership; concerns: unorganized, not open to new ideas, show-off. B uncooperative. C can make correct decisions quickly, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. D responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, moody, loner.\n\nEmily could bring up candidate C as a comparison: C's quick decision making and attention skills are strong, but communication difficulty and egocentric attitude are concerns. She could argue that A still stronger overall.\n\nThus produce public message: \"I agree with James, still recommend A. While we lack direct evidence on stress and crew interaction, A's anticipatory skills and leadership outweigh C's quick decisions but poor communication. Therefore A remains best.\" Also note need to get more info on A's stress resilience and crew cooperation.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate A.\n\nProceed."}
